==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|amuse|er|id2=agent noun|lang=en}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Someone who [[amuse]]s.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} One who [[divert]]s [[attention]], usually to [[distract]] or [[bewilder]], often for [[fraud]]ulent purposes; hence a [[cheat]], [[deceive]]r or [[thief]].
# {{lb|en|historical|early 19<sup>th</sup> century}} One of a class of rogues who carry snuff or dust in their pockets, which they throw into the eyes of people so as to enable their accomplices to rob them while pretending to help them.
#* '''1993''', Stella Cameron; ''Only by Your Touch'', Harpercollins, [http://books.google.com/books?id=VrApHV_ZWg8C&q=%22amuser%22#search_anchor page 88]:
#*: He should have knowed better than to tangle with you, Miss Lindsay. Where did you learn to be an '''amuser''', then?
#* '''2002''', various authors, ''Gangs of New York'' (film), Miramax Films, Entertainment Film Distributors:
#*: <small>BOSS TWEED</small> — No one important, necessarily. Average men will do. Back alley '''amusers''' with no affiliations.
#* '''2013''', Michelle Lovric; ''The Remedy'', Bloomsbury, [http://books.google.com/books?id=7thXKF_Tn7oC&pg=PT59&dq=%22amusers%22#v=onepage page 59]:
#*: Valentine watches the bunch of '''amusers''' close around the politician, the leader already dipping into his pocket for the snuff to fling into the eyes of their victim.

